# ARSENAL — Military History Museum

A modern, responsive website concept for a fictional military history museum, built with HTML and Tailwind CSS.

## About the Project

ARSENAL is a museum website concept designed to showcase historical military artifacts, uniforms, medals, vehicles, archival photographs, and the stories behind military history.

The project focuses on creating a professional, accessible, and responsive user interface while practising Tailwind CSS utility classes and modern web layout techniques.

## Project Goals

- Practise building real-world websites with Tailwind CSS.
- Improve responsive web design skills.
- Understand Flexbox, CSS Grid, spacing, typography, and breakpoints.
- Create reusable and consistent UI sections.
- Build a portfolio project that demonstrates practical frontend development skills.

## Technologies Used

- **HTML5** — Website structure and semantic markup.
- **Tailwind CSS v4** — Styling, responsive layouts, and UI design.
- **Tailwind Play CDN** — Development and learning setup.

> **Note:** The Tailwind CSS Play CDN is intended for development and learning. A production deployment should use a proper Tailwind CSS build setup.

## Pages

| Page | File | What it contains |
|---|---|---|
| Home | `index.html` | Hero, collection previews, history teaser, visit banner |
| Collections | `collections.html` | Overview of all three collections |
| Uniforms & Medals | `uniforms-medals.html` | Collection page |
| Vehicles & Aviation | `vehicles-aviation.html` | Collection page |
| Archives & Documents | `archives-documents.html` | Collection page |
| Our History | `our-history.html` | Museum introduction, mission, and a three-milestone timeline |
| Visit Us | `visit.html` | Placeholder address, opening hours, admission prices, and visitor guidelines |
| Contact | `contact.html` | Placeholder contact details and a demonstration form |

Every page shares the same navigation bar and footer.

## Design Direction

The website follows a modern, professional museum aesthetic.

- **Colors:** Deep navy `#101C2C`, warm ivory `#F7F5F0`, muted gold `#B99A5B`, slate gray `#64748B`, and white.
- **Typography:** Bold headings and clean, readable sans-serif body text.
- **Layout:** Spacious sections, responsive grids, and structured content.
- **Visual style:** Historical photography, subtle borders, and consistent spacing.

## Features

- Responsive layout for mobile, tablet, and desktop.
- Semantic HTML structure.
- Tailwind CSS utility-based styling.
- Mobile menu built with the HTML `<details>` element, so it needs no JavaScript.
- Accessible form labels, visible focus styles, a skip link, and descriptive link text.
- No JavaScript at all.

## Project Structure

```
arsenal-museum/
├── index.html
├── collections.html
├── uniforms-medals.html
├── vehicles-aviation.html
├── archives-documents.html
├── our-history.html
├── visit.html
├── contact.html
├── template.html
├── hero.jpg
├── uniforms.jpg
├── vehicles.jpg
├── archives.jpg
├── history.jpg
└── README.md
```

`template.html` is a blank page with the shared head, navigation, and footer. It is copied to start each new page.

## Getting Started

1. Clone or download this repository.
2. Open the project folder in your code editor.
3. Open `index.html` in your browser, or use the Live Server extension in VS Code.
4. Make sure you have an internet connection so the Tailwind Play CDN can load.
5. Edit the pages and images as you like.

## Images

Photos are kept in the same folder as the pages. File names are lowercase with no spaces, because GitHub Pages treats `Hero.jpg` and `hero.jpg` as different files. Only use photos with a free or public-domain license, and add the credit in the caption.

## Project Status

**Status:** Complete (frontend practice project)

Features such as working form submission, interactive filtering, and backend integrations are outside the scope of this project. The contact form is for demonstration only and does not send messages.

## Disclaimer

ARSENAL is a fictional educational museum concept created for frontend development practice. It is not a real institution. All addresses, opening hours, prices, and contact details are placeholders. Historical artifacts and imagery, if included, are intended for educational presentation.

